The `compute_checksum()` function calculates the checksum over the entire list to ensure data integrity. To optimize for speed and minimal memory footprint, I used simple linear search (find_rnti) and a basic singly-linked list implementation (the head pointer).

To handle potential discrepancies in data arrival times and incomplete data packets, this code integrates an external data source callback function `on_ue_info()`. Incoming data is buffered efficiently using a single linked-list structure. To balance between responsiveness to incoming data and the overhead of real-time updates, the choice was made to prioritize critical structures (UE information) over less critical ones (CRC_INDICATION_REL8_TAG) by processing them first in the `on_ue_info()` function. This helps ensure timely handling of urgent data without crashing due to buffer overflow or other related issues.

The code is designed with performance, memory usage, and real-time data integrity considerations in mind. The trade-off made here is that the linear search approach (find_rnti) can be slow when dealing with large lists, but it minimizes both memory usage and complexity. Overall, this solution should prove useful for managing a dynamically allocated list of NFAPI_CRC_INDICATION_PDU_T structures while balancing performance, memory usage, and real-time data integrity requirements under limited processing power and memory constraints.']

